<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byblos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jbeil, Jubail, Gibelet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byblos is an ancient port city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, known in Arabic as Jbeil or Jubail. Inhabited for some 7,000 years, it was a leading Phoenician city that traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the Greek word for book derives from its name. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> it was the lordship of Gibelet, held by the Genoese Embriaco family within the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byblos.jpg" alt="Byblos" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orient at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_Libanon_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Byblos</b>, called Gubla by its own people and in cuneiform texts, Gebal in the Hebrew Bible and Jbeil today, lies on a low headland on the coast of Lebanon about 35 kilometres north of Beirut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state sent expeditions for stone, amethyst, copper and turquoise to the Wadi Hammamat, the Wadi el-Hudi and Serabit el-Khadim in Sinai, and traded with <b>Byblos</b> and the Levant; a caravan of Asiatic traders is painted in the tomb of Khnumhotep II at Beni Hasan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…belonged to the Egyptian Empire and was ruled by Zimredda of Sidon, while the Amarna letters record the rivalry of the coastal cities, Abimilku of Tyre to the south and Rib-Hadda of <b>Byblos</b> to the north, as Egypt's grip loosened and the Hittites became overlords in the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to some sources it developed into a Tyro-Sidonian dialect, which spread across the Mediterranean through trade and colonisation and gave rise to Punic, and the ancient dialect of <b>Byblos</b>, known from only a few dozen inscriptions, which played no part in the expansion.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They never formed a single state, and their inscriptions name people by their cities, as Tyrians, Sidonians or men of Gebal, the city the Greeks called <b>Byblos</b>; the Hebrew Bible often calls them all Sidonians.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 2nd millennium BCE the Phoenician cities of Tyre, Sidon and <b>Byblos</b> traded across the Mediterranean and founded colonies.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
